{
  "id": "Compound::DB01067 - Gene::3767",
  "verbalization": "Glipizide, a sulfonylurea antidiabetic agent, is implicated in the relationship with Kcnj11 through multiple pathways that converge on the broader category of sulfonylurea compounds. The evidence suggests that Glipizide, through its inclusion in the sulfonylurea class, binds to Kcnj11 via interactions with Tolazamide, a member of the N-sulfonylurea subgroup. This binding is supported by the lowest common ancestor (LCA) relationship between Glipizide and Tolazamide, which links them to the sulfonylurea antidiabetic agent category. The mechanism of action appears to involve a direct interaction between Glipizide and Kcnj11, potentially modulating ion channel activity through the Kcnj11 protein. The presence of multiple sulfonylurea compounds, including Glimepiride, further reinforces the role of this class in mediating such interactions. The evidence is consistent with a mechanistic link between Glipizide and Kcnj11, though the exact nature of the interaction remains to be fully elucidated.",
